data.content:
Here are the improved MSTest assertions for your collection tests:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count);
    CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull(users);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));  // no built-in "all match predicate"
    var admin = users.SingleOrDefault(u => u.Role == "Admin");
    Assert.IsNotNull(admin);
}
```

**Key replacements:**

| Instead of | Use |
|---|---|
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count > 0)` | `Assert.AreNotEqual(0, users.Count)` or `CollectionAssert.That.IsNotEmpty()` (MSTest v3) |
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count() == 3)` | `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` — gives actual vs expected on failure |
| `Assert.IsTrue(obj != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(obj)` — clearer failure message |
| `Assert.IsTrue(list.Contains(x))` | `CollectionAssert.Contains(list, x)` |

**Other useful `CollectionAssert` methods:**

- `CollectionAssert.AreEqual(expected, actual)` — same order, same elements
- `CollectionAssert.AreEquivalent(expected, actual)` — same elements, any order
- `CollectionAssert.Contains(collection, element)`
- `CollectionAssert.DoesNotContain(collection, element)`
- `CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreUnique(collection)`
- `CollectionAssert.IsSubsetOf(subset, superset)`

The main benefit: when a test fails, these give you **diagnostic messages** (e.g., "Expected: 3. Actual: 2") instead of just "Assert.IsTrue failed."
